<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jana Gana Mana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11 December <a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/december-11">On this day: 11 December</a> · Also known as জনগণমন</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Jana Gana Mana&quot; is the first of five stanzas of a Bengali hymn, &quot;Bharoto Bhagyo Bidhata&quot;, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first Nobel laureate, completed on 11 December 1911. He wrote it in Sadhu Bhasha, a Sanskritised literary register of Bengali, as a hymn for the monotheistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a>, and set it himself in the raga Alhaiya Bilaval, possibly with help from his grand-nephew Dinendranath Tagore.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Jana Gana Mana</b>&quot; is the first of five stanzas of a Bengali hymn, &quot;Bharoto Bhagyo Bidhata&quot;, which Rabindranath Tagore, India's first Nobel laureate, completed on 11 December 1911.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a></li><li><blockquote>On 24 January 1950 the Constituent Assembly, with Rajendra Prasad presiding, declared &quot;<b>Jana Gana Mana</b>&quot; the national anthem and ruled that Vande Mataram, for the part it had played in the struggle for freedom, should be honoured equally with it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
